069 "q-336107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396916 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396916 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from {{field-1}} by the definition of a fundamental matrix.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-3}} by {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396916 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fcd6208-dde9-5e8a-b2c1-ff9f0dade0db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396916 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the definition of a fundamental matrix.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ In $\\text{L3}$ we have $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t),$ and in $\\text{L4}$ we have $Φ(t)c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).$ Since both expressions are equal to the same quantity, the transitive property of equality gives $x(t)=Φ(t)c.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L3 and L4}$ by the $\\text{the transitive property of equality}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186396920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396917 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "319112f1-c5cd-5b59-bc98-0fbaa63699a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396918 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c886e4d-ec71-526e-9fca-44411eb71087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396919 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3ee05532-dfb9-529e-b73c-929366f56b5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396920 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c690c80-9317-5e41